      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-184528",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. Then, $f(m, n) = 2m^{2}n - n^{2} + m$ is even only if $m$ and $n$ have the same parity.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the statement is as follows:\n\n$f(m, n)is even$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nConsider the following cases:\n\n**Case 1**. If $m$ is even, then $n$ is {{field-3}} and we have\n$2a + 1$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}\nwhere {{field-6}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-7}} into the expression $f(m, n)$, we can show that $f(m, n) =$ {{field-8}}, where $c$ is {{field-9}}.\n\nHence, since $f(m, n)$ {{field-10}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $f(m, n)$ is {{field-11}}.\n\n**Case 2**. If $m$ is odd, then $n$ is {{field-12}} and we have\n$2a + 1$ {{field-13}} {{field-14}}\nwhere {{field-15}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-16}} into the expression $f(m, n)$, we can show that $f(m, n) =$ {{field-17}}, where $c$ is {{field-18}}.\n\nHence, since $f(m, n)$ {{field-19}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $f(m, n)$ is {{field-20}}.\n\nFinally, combining the cases above, we conclude that $f(m, n)$ is {{field-21}} only if $m$ and $n$ have {{field-22}} parity."
