      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-184531",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. The evenness of $g(m, n) = m + n^{2} + 1$ implies that $m$ and $n$ have opposite parity.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the statement is as follows:\n\n$g(m, n)is odd$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nConsider the following cases:\n\n**Case 1**. If $m$ is even, then $n$ is {{field-3}}, and we have\n$2a + 1$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}\nwhere {{field-6}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-7}} into the expression $g(m, n)$, we can show that $g(m, n) =$ {{field-8}}, where $c$ is {{field-9}}.\n\nHence, since $g(m, n)$ {{field-10}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $g(m, n)$ is {{field-11}}.\n\n**Case 2**. If $m$ is odd, then $n$ is {{field-12}}, and we have\n$2a$ {{field-13}} {{field-14}}\nwhere {{field-15}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-16}} into the expression $g(m, n)$, we can show that $g(m, n) =$ {{field-17}}, where $c$ is {{field-18}}.\n\nHence, since $g(m, n)$ {{field-19}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $g(m, n)$ is {{field-20}}.\n\nFinally, combining the cases above, we conclude that the {{field-21}} of $g(m, n)$ implies that $m$ and $n$ have {{field-22}} parity."
